-- A `data` type with no per-language mapping, used inside a compiled pool.
--
-- When a type IS mapped (`data Rust => Shape = "Shape"`) the user's own file
-- declares the native form. With no mapping the compiler has to invent one,
-- and the name it invents is the morloc type's own. Getting that name wrong
-- is not a type error anywhere in morloc: it surfaces only as native code
-- that does not compile, or -- worse -- as a definition emitted under one
-- name and referred to under another.
--
-- A constructor only reaches a pool when it is an ARGUMENT to something the
-- pool runs. A bare export, a list literal and a `|`-pattern all stay in the
-- nexus, so the whole native path is invisible to them.
--
-- The type is declared in its own module, since that is where a program of
-- any size keeps its types, and a constructor has to survive the import.
--
-- `Box` is named to collide with Rust's own `Box`, which the generated
-- payload boxing uses: a user type may take any name the language allows.

module main
  ( enums
  , variants
  , roundTrip
  , enumsCpp
  , variantsCpp
  )

import .rustside
import .cppside
